1. Executive Summary
The goal of today’s changes was to achieve full functional and UI parity between the React Case Details Veteran Profile (case-details.tsx, case-details-read.tsx, case-details-edit.tsx) and the legacy application (VeteranInfo.js, _veteranProfile.cshtml, and VeteranService.cs). Specifically, we fixed address payload mapping, response parsing for backend C# Tuples, confirmation and alert modals, country display formatting, and toast notifications.

2. Detailed Changes by File & Feature
A. Address Payload Alignment (
case-details.tsx
/VA-MDE-App/webapp/mde-web-app/src/pages/case-search/case-details/case-details.tsx#L1265-L1310))
Change:
Corrected the mapping in handleSave:
Residency Address mapped to Address1, Address2, Address3, City, State, Zip, Phone, EmailAddress.
Communication Address mapped to ESR_Address1, ESR_Address2, ESR_Address3, ESR_City, ESR_State, ESR_ZipOrPostalCode, ESR_PrimaryPhone, ESR_EmailAddress.
Added ForceSave: false and VeteranNumber: formDataEdit.fileNumber.
Technical Reason / Legacy Alignment:
In the ASP.NET backend model (UpdateVeteranInfoAlternateAddressViewModel.cs) and VeteranService.cs, Address1..3 and Zip represent the Residency Address, while ESR_Address1..3 and ESR_ZipOrPostalCode represent the Communication Address.
Previously, the React payload had them swapped/inverted. This caused the backend to not detect changes in residency address and led to submission failures.
B. Response Parsing for Backend C# Tuples (extractBackendMessage) (
case-details.tsx
/VA-MDE-App/webapp/mde-web-app/src/pages/case-search/case-details/case-details.tsx#L1070-L1095))
Change:
Added a resilient message extraction helper extractBackendMessage(respData) that inspects Item1, item1, re, result, and data.
Technical Reason / Legacy Alignment:
The microservice endpoint at /Veteran/UpdateVeteranInfo returns an ASP.NET Tuple<string, string>, serialized into JSON as {"Item1": "...", "Item2": ""} (e.g. {"Item1": "RegionChange", "Item2": ""} or {"Item1": "ZIP Code not found.", "Item2": ""}).
Previously, React only checked res?.re (from old MVC controllers). Because re was undefined, it threw a runtime error caught by catch (err), resulting in the generic "Submission failed. Please try again." toast. Unpacking Item1 ensures direct compatibility with the microservice API.
